\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.4}{\ignorespaces A steep yield curve can be a forecast of rising rates, extra pay for holding long bonds, or both. Two readings of a steep yield curve. Forward rates above short rates can mean that short rates will rise, as the expectations hypothesis says, or that long bonds will earn more than short ones, a term premium. The tests in this chapter measure how much of each.}}{192}{figure.caption.47}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:07-readings}{{12.4}{192}{A steep yield curve can be a forecast of rising rates, extra pay for holding long bonds, or both. Two readings of a steep yield curve. Forward rates above short rates can mean that short rates will rise, as the expectations hypothesis says, or that long bonds will earn more than short ones, a term premium. The tests in this chapter measure how much of each}{figure.caption.47}{}}
